skill: Core Java and OOP

@ OOP Concepts | B
Which OOP principle hides an object's internal data and exposes it only through methods?
= Encapsulation
- Polymorphism
- Inheritance
- Overloading
? Encapsulation bundles data with methods and restricts direct access, typically using private fields and public getters and setters.

@ OOP Concepts | B
Which keyword prevents a class from being extended by another class?
= final
- static
- abstract
- private
? A final class cannot be subclassed. An abstract class, in contrast, is designed to be extended.

@ OOP Concepts | I
Method overloading is an example of which kind of polymorphism?
= Compile-time polymorphism
- Runtime polymorphism
- Multiple inheritance
- Encapsulation
? Overloaded methods are resolved by the compiler using the argument types, so it is compile-time (static) polymorphism. Overriding is runtime polymorphism.

@ OOP Concepts | I
Which statement about abstract classes is true?
= They can contain both abstract and concrete methods
- They can be instantiated directly with new
- Every method in them must be abstract
- They cannot have constructors
? An abstract class may mix implemented and abstract methods and can have constructors, but it cannot be instantiated on its own.

@ OOP Concepts | I
Which statement about interfaces in Java 8 and later is correct?
= Interfaces can contain default and static methods with a body
- Interfaces can declare constructors
- Interfaces can hold non-static, non-final instance variables
- A class uses the extends keyword to inherit from an interface
? Java 8 added default and static methods to interfaces. Fields in interfaces are implicitly public static final, and classes use implements.

@ OOP Concepts | I
What does this code print?
  class A { void show() { System.out.println("A"); } }
  class B extends A { void show() { System.out.println("B"); } }
  A obj = new B();
  obj.show();
= B
- A
- AB
- A compile-time error
? The overridden method is chosen at runtime from the actual object type (B), not from the reference type (A). This is runtime polymorphism.

@ OOP Concepts | A
What does this code print?
  class P { static void f() { System.out.println("P"); } }
  class C extends P { static void f() { System.out.println("C"); } }
  P p = new C();
  p.f();
= P
- C
- PC
- A runtime exception
? Static methods are hidden, not overridden. The call is bound at compile time using the reference type P.

@ OOP Concepts | I
Which is the most restrictive access modifier that still lets a subclass in a different package access a member?
= protected
- private
- default (package-private)
- A subclass in a different package can never access it
? protected members are accessible in the same package and in subclasses, even in other packages. private and default do not allow that.

@ OOP Concepts | A
What happens if a subclass constructor does not explicitly call super(...)?
= The compiler inserts a call to the superclass no-argument constructor
- The superclass constructor is never called
- The code fails to compile in every case
- super() is called after the subclass constructor body finishes
? Every constructor begins with an implicit super() call unless it calls this(...) or super(...) explicitly. If the superclass has no no-arg constructor the code will not compile.

@ OOP Concepts | A
Which statement about the equals() and hashCode() contract is correct?
= If two objects are equal according to equals(), they must return the same hashCode()
- If two objects have the same hashCode(), equals() must return true
- hashCode() must return a different value for every object
- Overriding equals() alone is always enough for objects used as HashMap keys
? Equal objects must have equal hash codes. The reverse is not required, because different objects may collide.

@ OOP Concepts | B
Can a Java class extend more than one class directly?
= No, Java allows a class to extend only one class
- Yes, by listing classes separated by commas after extends
- Yes, but only if all of them are abstract
- Yes, but only inside the same package
? Java does not support multiple inheritance of classes. A class may implement multiple interfaces instead.

@ Collections | B
Which of these collections does NOT allow duplicate elements?
= HashSet
- ArrayList
- LinkedList
- Vector
? A Set stores unique elements only. Lists allow duplicates.

@ Collections | I
Which Map implementation preserves the insertion order of its keys?
= LinkedHashMap
- HashMap
- TreeMap
- None of them preserves any order
? LinkedHashMap keeps entries in insertion order. HashMap has no guaranteed order and TreeMap keeps keys sorted.

@ Collections | I
What is the average-case time complexity of get(key) in a HashMap?
= O(1)
- O(n)
- O(log n)
- O(n log n)
? A HashMap computes a hash to find the bucket directly, so lookups are constant time on average.

@ Collections | I
What does this code print?
  List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>(List.of(1, 2, 3, 4));
  list.remove(1);
  System.out.println(list);
= [1, 3, 4]
- [2, 3, 4]
- [1, 2, 4]
- [1, 2, 3, 4]
? remove(int index) is chosen for an int argument, so the element at index 1 (the value 2) is removed.

@ Collections | A
What typically happens if you remove elements from an ArrayList inside an enhanced for loop over that same list?
= A ConcurrentModificationException is thrown
- The removal works safely every time
- The code does not compile
- The loop silently skips the removal
? The iterator detects a structural modification made outside of itself and fails fast. Use Iterator.remove() or removeIf instead.

@ Collections | I
Which statement about HashMap is correct?
= It allows one null key and multiple null values
- It allows no null keys or values
- It is thread-safe by default
- It keeps its keys sorted
? HashMap permits a single null key and any number of null values. It is not synchronized.

@ Exception Handling | B
Which block executes whether or not an exception occurs (unless the JVM exits)?
= finally
- catch
- try
- throws
? The finally block is intended for cleanup code and runs after try and catch.

@ Exception Handling | B
Which of these is a checked exception?
= IOException
- NullPointerException
- ArithmeticException
- ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException
? Checked exceptions such as IOException must be caught or declared. The others are unchecked (RuntimeException subclasses).

@ Exception Handling | I
What does this code print?
  try {
    int x = 10 / 0;
  } catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.print("A");
  } finally {
    System.out.print("F");
  }
= AF
- A
- F
- FA
? Division by zero throws ArithmeticException, so the catch block prints A, then finally prints F.

@ Exception Handling | I
Which keyword is used to explicitly throw an exception object?
= throw
- throws
- raise
- try
? throw raises an exception instance. throws only declares that a method may throw one.

@ Exception Handling | A
What does this code print?
  static int f() {
    try { return 1; }
    finally { System.out.print("F"); }
  }
  System.out.println(f());
= F1
- 1F
- 1
- F
? The finally block runs before the method actually returns, printing F first. Then println prints the returned value 1.

@ Exception Handling | A
Which rule applies to a multi-catch block such as catch (IOException | SQLException e)?
= The listed exception types must not be subclasses of each other
- The variable e can be reassigned inside the block
- Only unchecked exceptions are allowed
- It requires at least three alternatives
? In a multi-catch the alternatives cannot be related by inheritance, and the parameter is implicitly final.

@ Java 8 Features | I
Which functional interface takes one argument and returns a boolean?
= Predicate
- Consumer
- Supplier
- Runnable
? Predicate<T> has a test(T) method that returns boolean. Consumer returns nothing and Supplier takes no argument.

@ Java 8 Features | I
What does this code compute?
  List<Integer> n = List.of(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6);
  int r = n.stream().filter(x -> x % 2 == 0).mapToInt(x -> x * x).sum();
= 56
- 91
- 12
- 21
? The even numbers are 2, 4 and 6, and their squares sum to 4 + 16 + 36 = 56.
